(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 41-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for tricuspid valve endocarditis. On day 2 he develops a new cough, and chest imaging shows several new scattered lesions in both lungs. Which complication best explains these findings?","choices":["Aspiration of vomit","Septic pulmonary emboli","Drug-induced lung injury","Left heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He has tricuspid valve endocarditis, a right-sided vegetation, and on day 2 develops a new cough with several scattered lesions in both lungs. The lung is a listed embolic complication of infective endocarditis, along with the spleen, kidney and liver, and multiple new lesions fit septic emboli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with acute endocarditis asks why the team is so worried when she felt well a week ago. Which statement about untreated acute endocarditis is accurate?","choices":["It can be fatal within weeks","It resolves without treatment","It smolders harmlessly for years","It is mainly a lung infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She felt well a week ago, the rapid onset typical of acute disease (typically 3-10 days, with high fevers and systemic toxicity). A virulent organism rapidly damages cardiac structures and seeds extracardiac sites, and if untreated, acute endocarditis progresses to death within weeks. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Two separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus, a transesophageal echocardiogram shows no vegetation, and he has no predisposing heart condition, no drug use and no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Definite endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out","No Duke criteria met"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He meets one major criterion, a typical organism (Staphylococcus aureus) in two separate blood cultures, and one minor criterion, fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). The negative transesophageal echocardiogram and absent predisposition, vascular and immunologic findings add nothing, so one major plus one minor is possible endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis brings an old wallet card that lists clindamycin for her dental prophylaxis. She has a penicillin allergy. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Clindamycin is dropped","Keep using clindamycin","Switch to amoxicillin","She no longer needs any"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection. Her previous endocarditis still qualifies her, so with a penicillin allergy she receives azithromycin or clarithromycin, doxycycline, or cephalexin if her reaction was not anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with native mitral valve endocarditis after dental work has blood cultures growing penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. Which antibiotic is a directed choice?","choices":["Daptomycin alone","Penicillin G or ceftriaxone","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Rifampin plus gentamicin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her cultures grow penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci after dental work, and viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone, with or without gentamicin, or vancomycin. Adding gentamicin allows a 2-week regimen only for uncomplicated disease with rapid response and no chronic kidney disease. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 9-year-old with unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is scheduled for a tonsillectomy. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Give antibiotics afterward instead","Cancel the tonsillectomy","Give prophylaxis before surgery","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease, including palliative shunts and conduits, is a high-risk pediatric condition, and tonsillectomy is an invasive respiratory procedure with mucosal incision. Both conditions are met, so prophylaxis is given as a single dose 30-60 minutes before surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve has a fever of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) and Osler nodes (painful red nodules on the pads of her fingers). Under the modified Duke criteria, which category do the Osler nodes fall into?","choices":["Predisposition","Major blood culture criterion","Immunologic phenomenon","Vascular phenomenon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are an immunologic phenomenon in the modified Duke criteria, a minor criterion shared with glomerulonephritis, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor. Her other findings count separately: the prosthetic valve is predisposition, and 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) meets the fever criterion of 38.0 °C or higher. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has subacute endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow Eikenella corrodens, a member of the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Rifampin","Ceftriaxone","Nafcillin","Vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her cultures grow Eikenella corrodens, a HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism, and HACEK endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone 2 g intravenously once daily for 4 weeks. The listed alternative is ampicillin-sulbactam for 4 weeks. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who lives at home has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur. He has had no recent hospital stay, procedure or urinary symptoms, and no primary source of infection is found. Two separate blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. How do these cultures count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not counted until echocardiography","A major criterion","A minor criterion","Excluded as contaminants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired enterococci in the absence of a primary focus count as a typical organism for endocarditis. He lives at home, has had no hospital stay, procedure or urinary source, and two separate cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis, so the major blood culture criterion is met. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with native aortic valve endocarditis and a large vegetation asks whether a blood thinner would prevent a stroke. He has no other reason for anticoagulation. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["Starting heparin or warfarin","Continuing his antibiotics","Monitoring renal function","Consulting infectious disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is not beneficial in patients with native valve infection; it is only controversial in patients with prosthetic valves. He has native aortic valve endocarditis and no other reason for anticoagulation, so heparin or warfarin should not be started to prevent a stroke. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever has four sets of blood cultures drawn. One set grows coagulase-negative staphylococci, and the other three are negative. How should this single positive culture be counted under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Excluded as a likely contaminant","As the major blood culture criterion","As a vascular phenomenon","As definite proof of endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A single positive culture for coagulase-negative staphylococci, with the other three sets negative, is excluded from the criteria because coagulase-negative staphylococci (like diphtheroids) are common culture contaminants. It counts as neither a major criterion nor the minor microbiologic criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a prosthetic aortic valve is scheduled for a screening colonoscopy and has no active infection. She asks whether she needs antibiotics beforehand. Which advice is most accurate?","choices":["Amoxicillin 30-60 minutes before","A 7-day antibiotic course","Intravenous vancomycin before","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal and genitourinary procedures, including colonoscopy, upper endoscopy and cystoscopy, do not require endocarditis prophylaxis. Her prosthetic aortic valve does not change this, because the procedure itself does not qualify and she has no active infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman is ready for discharge on day 9 of a planned 6-week course of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis. She is deconditioned and cannot yet walk safely on her own. Which part of the multidisciplinary plan addresses this?","choices":["Immediate valve surgery","Stopping the antibiotics","Rehabilitation placement","Addiction medicine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The multidisciplinary team plans disposition, and rehabilitation is part of that plan for a deconditioned 83-year-old who cannot walk safely. Infectious disease continues to oversee the remaining weeks of antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F) and Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages) on fundoscopy, meeting three minor Duke criteria. He has received no antibiotics. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure him that it is not endocarditis","Order a chest radiograph and discharge","Draw blood cultures from separate sites","Start oral amoxicillin and recheck later"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With three minor criteria (injection drug use, fever of 38.8 °C and Roth spots) and no antibiotics given, the next step is three sets of blood cultures from separate sites, before empiric therapy. A typical organism in the cultures would add a major criterion, and one major plus three minor criteria is definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and malaise that began a week after a dental extraction. A new murmur of mitral regurgitation is heard. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn. Which organism are they most likely to grow?","choices":["Enterococci","Candida species","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three weeks of low-grade fever and malaise is a subacute course, and its onset a week after a dental extraction points to the oral cavity, the source of viridans streptococci. Viridans streptococci are a classic subacute organism and a leading cause of native valve endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man will finish 5 more weeks of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis after discharge. Which consultant manages his antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring?","choices":["Addiction medicine","Dermatology","Infectious disease","Cardiothoracic surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Infectious disease is consulted for antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring pending disposition, so it oversees his remaining 5 weeks of intravenous antibiotics after discharge. Cardiothoracic surgery and addiction medicine have separate roles in the multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has 5 days of high fever and rigors. Transthoracic imaging shows a large vegetation on the tricuspid valve, and three sets of blood cultures are drawn from separate sites. Which organism are the cultures most likely to grow?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococcus faecalis","Candida albicans","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus, a skin organism, causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases in which the tricuspid valve is infected. Her 5 days of high fever and rigors with a large tricuspid vegetation fit that pattern. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a malar rash, painful joints and a positive antinuclear antibody test has a new murmur. She has no fever, repeated blood cultures are negative, and echocardiography shows small sterile-appearing valve vegetations. Which is the most likely cause of her valve lesion?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Acute infective endocarditis","Device endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has features of systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash, painful joints, positive antinuclear antibody), and lupus may present with non-infective Libman-Sacks endocarditis. No fever, repeatedly negative blood cultures and sterile-appearing vegetations fit this autoimmune mimic rather than infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve is booked for a diagnostic bronchoscopy. No biopsy or mucosal incision is planned. Which advice about endocarditis prophylaxis is most accurate?","choices":["Required before every bronchoscopy","A week of oral amoxicillin","Intravenous vancomycin first","None is needed for this procedure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchoscopy does not require endocarditis prophylaxis unless it involves incision of the respiratory mucosa, such as a biopsy. His is diagnostic with no biopsy or incision planned, so no antibiotic is given despite the prosthetic mitral valve; respiratory prophylaxis is reserved for procedures that incise or biopsy mucosa. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"An 85-year-old man with degenerative aortic valve disease presents with fever and a new murmur. His family is surprised that a heart infection could occur at his age. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Age lowers the risk","Over-60s are over half of cases","It occurs mainly in drug users","It occurs mainly in children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk is increased among the elderly, and people over 60 account for more than 50% of cases, although endocarditis can occur at any age. His degenerative aortic valve disease is now one of the leading predisposing conditions in high-income countries. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with subacute endocarditis has a fundoscopic examination that shows Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages). What process causes these spots?","choices":["Optic nerve swelling from pressure","Hypertensive retinal changes","Vasculitis of small retinal arteries","Retinal detachment from trauma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Roth spots are white-centered hemorrhages produced by vasculitis affecting the small arteries of the retina. In this man with subacute endocarditis they are the eye finding of the disease, alongside exam findings such as splinter hemorrhages, petechiae, Osler nodes and Janeway lesions. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a fever, a new murmur and Janeway lesions (painless macules on her soles), and two blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Her transesophageal echocardiogram is read as normal. Which conclusion is correct?","choices":["She has a skin infection instead","Endocarditis is not ruled out","Endocarditis is now excluded","The cultures were contaminated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, but a normal study does not necessarily rule out endocarditis. Her fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions and two blood cultures growing Staphylococcus aureus keep the diagnosis open, so workup and treatment continue. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 9 days after hip surgery. She develops a new fever, and two sets of blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus; she has no indwelling catheter, and examination finds no wound or other source. Which diagnosis must be suspected?","choices":["Contamination of both culture sets","Postoperative atelectasis","Drug fever from antibiotics","Health care-associated endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has been in the hospital for 9 days and now has fever with two sets of positive blood cultures and no apparent source (no catheter, no wound). Health care-associated endocarditis is less common but should be suspected in exactly this situation, and 25-35% of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 7-year-old with congenital heart disease is admitted with viridans streptococcal endocarditis. Which approach to antibiotic therapy is most appropriate?","choices":["Intramuscular, fixed adult dose","Oral, for one week","Observation without antibiotics","Intravenous, weight-based, prolonged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management in children is essentially the same as in adults: antibiotics that are prolonged, bactericidal, parenteral (intravenous, not intramuscular), pathogen-directed and weight-based. This 7-year-old's dose is calculated by weight, given intravenously and continued for a prolonged course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["Low-flow ischemia from heart block","A side effect of sedation","Septic cerebral emboli","A primary bacterial meningitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever, a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae suggest endocarditis, and multiple small scattered brain lesions fit septic emboli. Cerebral complications include ischemic stroke, abscess, intracranial hemorrhage and meningitis, and neurologic deficits are a recognized manifestation of endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 4-month-old with tachycardia, poor feeding, respiratory distress and hypotension is being worked up for sepsis. A new murmur is heard. Why must endocarditis be considered even though the presentation looks like sepsis?","choices":["Infants show classic Osler nodes","Infants have marked joint pain","Infants rarely develop sepsis","Infants present nonspecifically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In neonates and infants, endocarditis presents nonspecifically and is often indistinguishable from sepsis or heart failure: feeding difficulty, respiratory distress, tachycardia, hypotension and a new or changing murmur. This 4-month-old has exactly that picture, so the new murmur must not be dismissed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in her shoulders and hips, fatigue and a low-grade fever. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are high, but no murmur is heard and three sets of blood cultures are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Subacute endocarditis","Atrial myxoma","Antiphospholipid syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Six weeks of shoulder and hip aching and stiffness, fatigue, low-grade fever and a high erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein fit polymyalgia rheumatica, which mimics endocarditis through myalgia and elevated inflammatory markers. The absent murmur and three negative culture sets argue against endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve is having a gum biopsy. She is not allergic to penicillin, but she cannot swallow pills after a recent stroke. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["No prophylaxis","Ampicillin by injection","Oral amoxicillin","Injected clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has a prosthetic mitral valve and a gum biopsy that manipulates gingival tissue, so prophylaxis is indicated. With no penicillin allergy but no ability to take oral medication, the regimen is ampicillin 2 g intramuscularly or intravenously within 30-60 minutes before the procedure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has enterococcal endocarditis of the aortic valve after recurrent urinary infections. Which outcome is he at notable risk for?","choices":["Septic lung emboli","Needing heart surgery","Spontaneous cure","Lowest death rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His enterococcal aortic valve endocarditis is difficult to treat because of antibiotic resistance, and major complications with need for valve replacement occur in about 30-40% of patients. Needing heart surgery is the notable risk for this organism. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man on chemotherapy has Candida endocarditis confirmed on blood cultures and echocardiography. Which referral is most appropriate in addition to infectious disease?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Physical therapy","Dermatology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is an indication for surgery because it is difficult to cure with medications alone. His Candida endocarditis therefore calls for cardiothoracic surgery, which evaluates surgery and anticoagulation needs, while infectious disease manages his drug therapy and monitoring. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
